WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.750
STEPHANIE R. WONG:
Cloud Functions 2nd gen

00:00:01.750 --> 00:00:04.290
helps developers achieve more
when deploying their code

00:00:04.290 --> 00:00:06.240
to a serverless environment.

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:08.820
Longer processing times,
faster response times

00:00:08.820 --> 00:00:12.090
with greater cost efficiency,
and native integrations

00:00:12.090 --> 00:00:14.040
with dozens of event sources.

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:16.050
Features like these
enable developers

00:00:16.050 --> 00:00:20.430
to take advantage of serverless
with more diverse workloads.

00:00:20.430 --> 00:00:22.710
I introduced some of this
new functionality embedded

00:00:22.710 --> 00:00:25.050
into the new generation
of Cloud Functions.

00:00:25.050 --> 00:00:26.820
But today, let's
take a deeper dive

00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:28.500
into how these
functions in 2nd gen

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:31.650
work and walk through how
developers can take advantage

00:00:31.650 --> 00:00:33.810
of this functionality.

00:00:33.810 --> 00:00:36.300
These new features in
Cloud Function 2nd gen

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:40.680
can be categorized into two
key themes, enhanced underlying

00:00:40.680 --> 00:00:44.370
infrastructure and greater
event integrations based

00:00:44.370 --> 00:00:46.890
on the open source
spec CloudEvents,

00:00:46.890 --> 00:00:48.900
all while maintaining
the same developer

00:00:48.900 --> 00:00:51.600
contract you're familiar
with in Cloud Functions.

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:54.450
Write your code, deploy it,
and let the serverless platform

00:00:54.450 --> 00:00:56.130
handle the rest.

00:00:56.130 --> 00:00:59.640
It is worth sharing what powers
the improved infrastructure

00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:01.650
of this new generation.

00:01:01.650 --> 00:01:05.290
2nd gen leverages Cloud
Run under the hood,

00:01:05.290 --> 00:01:08.170
which is Google's container
as a service platform.

00:01:08.170 --> 00:01:10.860
This is where new
2nd gen functionality

00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:14.370
such as concurrency and
traffic splitting comes from.

00:01:14.370 --> 00:01:17.310
The beauty of it is that you
don't have to build and bring

00:01:17.310 --> 00:01:19.860
a container image to
Cloud Function 2nd gen

00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:24.030
as your ticket for admission,
just to use a new feature set.

00:01:24.030 --> 00:01:26.400
Cloud Functions 2nd gen
maintains the same developer

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:28.710
experience as its
preceding generation.

00:01:28.710 --> 00:01:30.870
Just bring your code and
the serverless platform

00:01:30.870 --> 00:01:32.380
will handle the rest.

00:01:32.380 --> 00:01:34.380
All right, let's now dig
into how these features

00:01:34.380 --> 00:01:36.240
work, starting with
one of the ones

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:39.450
we heard developers ask
for the most, running

00:01:39.450 --> 00:01:42.450
functions with a lot
longer execution times.

00:01:42.450 --> 00:01:45.300
Event-driven functions can
now run an additional minute

00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:47.190
compared to the
previous generation,

00:01:47.190 --> 00:01:50.220
and HTTP functions can
run more than six times

00:01:50.220 --> 00:01:52.170
longer in second generation.

00:01:52.170 --> 00:01:55.140
This means that you can now
bring more complex functions

00:01:55.140 --> 00:01:58.740
that perform more intensive
processing to Cloud Functions.

00:01:58.740 --> 00:02:02.880
To demonstrate, we'll run this
function using Cloud Functions

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:05.100
Python 3.8 runtime.

00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:10.030
It may not be complex, but it'll
run for the maximum timeout.

00:02:10.030 --> 00:02:11.520
It's important to
note that the max

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:14.610
timeout is supported across
all language runtimes, not just

00:02:14.610 --> 00:02:15.638
Python.

00:02:15.638 --> 00:02:17.430
We'll deploy this code
as a function called

00:02:17.430 --> 00:02:20.790
function-timeout and invoke it.

00:02:20.790 --> 00:02:23.640
With the function invoked, we
can see the logs from our code

00:02:23.640 --> 00:02:26.670
directly in the Cloud
Functions console.

00:02:26.670 --> 00:02:28.620
The function counts
down minute by minute

00:02:28.620 --> 00:02:33.490
until it returns a 504 once
the timeout has been hit.

00:02:33.490 --> 00:02:36.760
We can also view execution times
for our long-running functions

00:02:36.760 --> 00:02:39.700
in dashboards provided
by Cloud Functions.

00:02:39.700 --> 00:02:42.970
And if we look at how we
configure this max timeout,

00:02:42.970 --> 00:02:45.880
we can do this by appending
the timeout parameter

00:02:45.880 --> 00:02:50.080
at deploy time to override the
default timeout of one minute.

00:02:50.080 --> 00:02:53.140
Now let's briefly talk about
the greater amount of resources

00:02:53.140 --> 00:02:57.400
that are now available to
your functions in 2nd gen.

00:02:57.400 --> 00:03:01.090
Previously you were only able
to specify how much memory you

00:03:01.090 --> 00:03:03.640
wanted to make available
to your instances

00:03:03.640 --> 00:03:08.350
up to roughly 8 gigabytes, with
only up to 2 CPUs per function

00:03:08.350 --> 00:03:09.280
instance.

00:03:09.280 --> 00:03:12.640
Now you're able to specify
double the amount of CPUs,

00:03:12.640 --> 00:03:15.670
up to 4 vCPUs to
allocate to your function

00:03:15.670 --> 00:03:18.100
while also doubling the
amount of memory you can make

00:03:18.100 --> 00:03:20.620
available, up to 16 gigabytes.

00:03:20.620 --> 00:03:23.020
This can help
resource-intensive workloads

00:03:23.020 --> 00:03:24.880
that need to
process large files,

00:03:24.880 --> 00:03:27.250
write to an in-memory
file system,

00:03:27.250 --> 00:03:29.680
or even perform
parallel processing.

00:03:29.680 --> 00:03:31.510
Let's move on to
traffic-splitting.

00:03:31.510 --> 00:03:34.150
In second generation, Cloud
Functions as a platform

00:03:34.150 --> 00:03:36.640
provides for you automatic
traffic splitting

00:03:36.640 --> 00:03:38.650
between various
revisions of your code.

00:03:38.650 --> 00:03:41.050
With this functionality,
you can feel safer

00:03:41.050 --> 00:03:42.880
rolling out new
versions of your code,

00:03:42.880 --> 00:03:44.980
knowing 2nd gen
can make patterns

00:03:44.980 --> 00:03:47.530
like canary deployments
or A/B testing

00:03:47.530 --> 00:03:49.870
straightforward to implement.

00:03:49.870 --> 00:03:52.390
Take this example
in the diagram where

00:03:52.390 --> 00:03:55.990
we have a new version of our
code that we have deployed.

00:03:55.990 --> 00:03:57.790
Rather than
immediately promoting

00:03:57.790 --> 00:03:59.870
it to serve all
production traffic,

00:03:59.870 --> 00:04:03.820
we can gradually begin sending
traffic to it using 2nd gen,

00:04:03.820 --> 00:04:05.500
starting with 10%.

00:04:05.500 --> 00:04:07.870
Let's simulate the scenario
by actually deploying

00:04:07.870 --> 00:04:09.820
a simple HTTP function.

00:04:09.820 --> 00:04:11.380
There may be a case
in which we are

00:04:11.380 --> 00:04:14.350
using Cloud Function support
for environment variables

00:04:14.350 --> 00:04:17.600
to flip a new feature
in our code on or off.

00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:20.500
But in this example, let's
just set the variable version

00:04:20.500 --> 00:04:21.985
to 1.0.0.

00:04:21.985 --> 00:04:23.890
To deploy the next
version of the code,

00:04:23.890 --> 00:04:25.870
we supply the new
environment variable

00:04:25.870 --> 00:04:29.560
value to reflect that we
are deploying version 2.0.0.

00:04:29.560 --> 00:04:31.540
Once we have both versions
of the code deployed

00:04:31.540 --> 00:04:33.160
to Cloud Functions,
we can actually

00:04:33.160 --> 00:04:35.230
see that each revision
we've deployed

00:04:35.230 --> 00:04:37.600
is being tracked in the
underlying infrastructure,

00:04:37.600 --> 00:04:40.010
Cloud Run.

00:04:40.010 --> 00:04:43.160
Now that Cloud Run has shared
with us the unique revision

00:04:43.160 --> 00:04:45.620
IDs for our two versions
of our function,

00:04:45.620 --> 00:04:48.020
we can now declaratively
define how traffic

00:04:48.020 --> 00:04:50.060
should flow to each revision.

00:04:50.060 --> 00:04:52.970
In our case here, we want
to send 90% of traffic

00:04:52.970 --> 00:04:56.340
to the first version and 10% of
traffic to the second version.

00:04:56.340 --> 00:04:58.370
We do so by providing
a single parameter

00:04:58.370 --> 00:05:00.410
to the underlying
Cloud Run service,

00:05:00.410 --> 00:05:04.160
backing the Cloud Function
with those traffic percentages.

00:05:04.160 --> 00:05:07.550
Once we begin issuing requests
to the function's URL,

00:05:07.550 --> 00:05:09.920
we can see that we get
a simple response that

00:05:09.920 --> 00:05:12.920
shows what version of
the app we are accessing.

00:05:12.920 --> 00:05:15.470
The majority of our traffic
is routed to version 1,

00:05:15.470 --> 00:05:18.710
and a small minority of our
traffic is routed to version 2,

00:05:18.710 --> 00:05:21.740
all configured by
simple gcloud commands.

00:05:21.740 --> 00:05:23.485
Speaking of functions
handling requests,

00:05:23.485 --> 00:05:24.860
let's cover one
more feature that

00:05:24.860 --> 00:05:27.770
comes from the new underlying
infrastructure in 2nd gen,

00:05:27.770 --> 00:05:29.270
concurrency.

00:05:29.270 --> 00:05:32.150
Previously in Cloud Functions,
each instance of your code

00:05:32.150 --> 00:05:34.280
would handle a single request.

00:05:34.280 --> 00:05:36.380
This means that as
more requests come in,

00:05:36.380 --> 00:05:39.470
the more compute time
your function uses.

00:05:39.470 --> 00:05:42.290
In Cloud Functions 2nd
gen, however, you're

00:05:42.290 --> 00:05:44.930
now able to define
concurrency, that is,

00:05:44.930 --> 00:05:47.450
having one instance of your
code handle multiple requests

00:05:47.450 --> 00:05:48.650
at the same time.

00:05:48.650 --> 00:05:51.623
Let's deploy a simple
Python 3.8 function.

00:05:51.623 --> 00:05:53.040
Once we've deployed
this function,

00:05:53.040 --> 00:05:54.890
we can then utilize the
underlying Cloud Run

00:05:54.890 --> 00:05:57.860
functionality and update
that Cloud Function to handle

00:05:57.860 --> 00:06:00.350
more than one concurrent
request at a time, which

00:06:00.350 --> 00:06:05.450
is the default. In this case,
we'll set this value to 8.

00:06:05.450 --> 00:06:07.970
We can then use the hey
utility to demonstrate

00:06:07.970 --> 00:06:10.370
how Cloud Functions
will handle concurrency.

00:06:10.370 --> 00:06:14.030
We'll send 8 requests at once
from 8 different workers.

00:06:14.030 --> 00:06:16.760
In our demo, our Cloud
Function only accepts requests

00:06:16.760 --> 00:06:20.330
from authenticated users, so we
will obtain our identity token

00:06:20.330 --> 00:06:22.700
to pass in our
requests using gcloud.

00:06:22.700 --> 00:06:24.350
And finally, we'll
send the request

00:06:24.350 --> 00:06:27.380
to the URL provided to
us by Cloud Functions.

00:06:27.380 --> 00:06:30.880
The code for this function
simply sleeps for 15 seconds.

00:06:30.880 --> 00:06:33.380
You'll see we deploy the two
revisions, the more recent with

00:06:33.380 --> 00:06:35.060
concurrency at 8,
and the one before it

00:06:35.060 --> 00:06:36.620
with concurrency at 1.

00:06:36.620 --> 00:06:38.930
When we hit the function
with concurrency at 1,

00:06:38.930 --> 00:06:42.680
we see 1 request respond
quickly and the remaining 7

00:06:42.680 --> 00:06:43.970
take much longer.

00:06:43.970 --> 00:06:47.240
This is because we only had one
warm instance of our function.

00:06:47.240 --> 00:06:50.150
Every other request required
a new instance of our function

00:06:50.150 --> 00:06:52.580
to spin up, which takes time.

00:06:52.580 --> 00:06:54.890
However, after updating
concurrency to 8,

00:06:54.890 --> 00:06:57.770
we see that all our
requests complete roughly

00:06:57.770 --> 00:07:00.510
within the expected 15 seconds.

00:07:00.510 --> 00:07:03.260
This is because we had one warm
instance, that could handle

00:07:03.260 --> 00:07:06.350
all 8 requests simultaneously.

00:07:06.350 --> 00:07:08.120
This brings up a
pattern with which you

00:07:08.120 --> 00:07:10.220
can utilize concurrency with.

00:07:10.220 --> 00:07:12.350
In both first and
second generation,

00:07:12.350 --> 00:07:14.660
you have the ability to
define a minimum number

00:07:14.660 --> 00:07:17.840
of warm instances that are
always ready to handle requests

00:07:17.840 --> 00:07:19.680
to your function.

00:07:19.680 --> 00:07:23.210
This allows for lower latency
responses from your function,

00:07:23.210 --> 00:07:25.100
since you don't need
to pay the penalty

00:07:25.100 --> 00:07:26.930
from having your code start up.

00:07:26.930 --> 00:07:28.580
Now pair this with concurrency.

00:07:28.580 --> 00:07:31.100
Because a function instance can
handle more than one request

00:07:31.100 --> 00:07:34.130
at a time, you can now actually
use concurrency combined

00:07:34.130 --> 00:07:35.540
with minimum instances.

00:07:35.540 --> 00:07:37.920
This helps you reduce the
amount of min instances

00:07:37.920 --> 00:07:40.062
you may need to keep
around, while still ensuring

00:07:40.062 --> 00:07:42.020
that you can serve a
certain amount of requests

00:07:42.020 --> 00:07:43.550
with lower latency.

00:07:43.550 --> 00:07:46.310
Note that, in the example
we evaluated prior,

00:07:46.310 --> 00:07:49.760
all we have to do is add the
min instances parameter when

00:07:49.760 --> 00:07:51.800
deploying the Cloud Function.

00:07:51.800 --> 00:07:53.810
Cloud Function 2nd
gen can support up

00:07:53.810 --> 00:07:56.090
to 1,000 concurrent requests.

00:07:56.090 --> 00:07:58.490
But this maximum for
concurrent requests

00:07:58.490 --> 00:08:01.310
varies by language
runtime, so it's

00:08:01.310 --> 00:08:03.470
advised that you review
the documentation depending

00:08:03.470 --> 00:08:06.380
on what language you want
to use with Cloud Functions.

00:08:06.380 --> 00:08:08.870
Last but not least, let's
talk about the new eventing

00:08:08.870 --> 00:08:11.750
model in Cloud Functions
second generation.

00:08:11.750 --> 00:08:14.120
The previous generation
of Cloud Functions

00:08:14.120 --> 00:08:16.610
had a smaller set
of events that could

00:08:16.610 --> 00:08:20.870
trigger your code's execution,
and these were fairly limited.

00:08:20.870 --> 00:08:23.300
If you wanted to trigger
your code outside of the set,

00:08:23.300 --> 00:08:25.730
you typically had to wire
code to publish events

00:08:25.730 --> 00:08:27.470
to Cloud Pub/Sub.

00:08:27.470 --> 00:08:30.020
By integrating with
Eventarc, we ensure

00:08:30.020 --> 00:08:32.030
that every event that
triggers your code

00:08:32.030 --> 00:08:35.480
adheres to the CloudEvent
specification, an open standard

00:08:35.480 --> 00:08:37.470
for event data format.

00:08:37.470 --> 00:08:39.980
This means that your
code can consistently

00:08:39.980 --> 00:08:41.870
handle events in
the same format,

00:08:41.870 --> 00:08:44.300
regardless of what
the source is.

00:08:44.300 --> 00:08:46.160
With these Eventarc
integrations,

00:08:46.160 --> 00:08:48.530
over 90-plus event
sources using Cloud

00:08:48.530 --> 00:08:50.870
audit logs can now
be natively created

00:08:50.870 --> 00:08:53.150
as triggers for
event-driven functions.

00:08:53.150 --> 00:08:55.130
No custom code that you
need to wire together

00:08:55.130 --> 00:08:58.640
just to trigger your code
with Google Cloud events.

00:08:58.640 --> 00:09:02.270
Of course, you still can utilize
some custom event sources

00:09:02.270 --> 00:09:04.400
with Pub/Sub to
trigger your functions.

00:09:04.400 --> 00:09:06.320
In this demonstration
here, we are

00:09:06.320 --> 00:09:09.080
using the second generation
triggers based on Eventarc

00:09:09.080 --> 00:09:11.030
and audit logs to
process BigQuery event

00:09:11.030 --> 00:09:13.220
data every time a query is run.

00:09:13.220 --> 00:09:15.650
We'll use a query
in BigQuery now.

00:09:15.650 --> 00:09:19.100
This one will roughly
query 4.5 terabytes of data

00:09:19.100 --> 00:09:21.170
and take less than 30
seconds to complete.

00:09:21.170 --> 00:09:23.248
This is a fairly
large query, so we

00:09:23.248 --> 00:09:25.040
might want to use a
Cloud Function to react

00:09:25.040 --> 00:09:27.950
to its currents, maybe use
something like Notify Admins

00:09:27.950 --> 00:09:29.390
that it ran.

00:09:29.390 --> 00:09:32.420
With the query finished, we can
get back to our Cloud Function

00:09:32.420 --> 00:09:34.970
and see that we are writing
out the query information

00:09:34.970 --> 00:09:36.230
to standard out.

00:09:36.230 --> 00:09:38.420
But of course, we
could extend this

00:09:38.420 --> 00:09:41.650
to send a message to Slack, do
some processing, or anything

00:09:41.650 --> 00:09:43.120
else that we can imagine.

00:09:43.120 --> 00:09:45.040
BigQuery is just one example.

00:09:45.040 --> 00:09:46.910
With Cloud audit
logs and Eventarc,

00:09:46.910 --> 00:09:48.910
we can now trigger
off dozens and dozens

00:09:48.910 --> 00:09:50.680
of Google Cloud events.

00:09:50.680 --> 00:09:53.410
With all of these new features
within these two themes,

00:09:53.410 --> 00:09:56.110
Cloud Functions second
generation considerably

00:09:56.110 --> 00:09:58.450
improves the experience
for developers when

00:09:58.450 --> 00:10:00.880
compared to its
preceding version.

00:10:00.880 --> 00:10:02.740
But for all of
you who have built

00:10:02.740 --> 00:10:04.840
atop of the first
generation, it's

00:10:04.840 --> 00:10:07.630
important to note your function
in the first generation

00:10:07.630 --> 00:10:09.760
will continue to run as is.

00:10:09.760 --> 00:10:12.130
Over time, if you find the
features suit your needs

00:10:12.130 --> 00:10:13.720
or improve your
workflow, you can

00:10:13.720 --> 00:10:16.670
begin to deploy these functions
to the second generation.

00:10:16.670 --> 00:10:21.010
So thanks for spending this time
digging into Cloud Functions

00:10:21.010 --> 00:10:23.140
2nd gen. If you
want to learn more,

00:10:23.140 --> 00:10:25.780
then check out the
documentation and code labs

00:10:25.780 --> 00:10:28.662
to get familiar with the
improved developer experience.

00:10:28.662 --> 00:10:29.620
This is Stephanie Wong.

00:10:29.620 --> 00:10:31.530
See you next time.

